"Watling Street","at":[0.33,51.43],"r":1.5},{"name":"Ermine Street","at":[-1.34,53.68],"r":1.5},{"name":"Fosse Way","at":[-2.06,51.67],"r":1.5},{"name":"Dere Street","at":[-1.74,54.79],"r":1.5},{"name":"Via Maris","at":[32.67,31.01],"r":1.5},{"name":"Strata Diocletiana","at":[37.49,34.08],"r":1.5},{"name":"Via Militaris","at":[27.38,41.4],"r":1.5},{"name":"Via Agrippae","at":[4.79,44.67],"r":1.5},{"name":"Via Traiana","at":[16.28,41.19],"r":1.5}]},"kinds":{"republic":{"label":"Built under the Republic","color":"c3"},"empire":{"label":"Built under the emperors","color":"c2"}},"items":[{"title":"Via Appia","date":-311,"at":[13.31,41.33],"kind":"republic","text":"The censor Appius Claudius Caecus builds Rome's first great road, south from Rome through Tarracina and Fundi to Capua.","source":"Itiner-e: 198 km of sections dated 'Appius Claudius Caecus (312 BCE)'; bibliography: Appia Regina Viarum","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Aurelia","date":-240,"at":[10.96,42.75],"kind":"republic","text":"Gaius Aurelius Cotta lays out the coast road north-west from Rome into Etruria, towards Pisae.","source":"Itiner-e: 317 km of sections dated 'Gaius Aurelius Cotta (241 BCE)'; bibliography: BAtlas 42, BAtlas 43","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Flaminia","date":-219,"at":[12.78,43.16],"kind":"republic","text":"Gaius Flaminius builds the road across the Apennines from Rome to Fanum Fortunae and Ariminum on the Adriatic.","source":"Itiner-e: 307 km of sections dated 'Gaius Flaminius Nepos (220-219 BCE)'; bibliography: BAtlas 42","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Aemilia","date":-186,"at":[11.32,44.5],"kind":"republic","text":"Marcus Aemilius Lepidus runs a straight road from Ariminum to Placentia, along the edge of the Po plain.","source":"Itiner-e: 259 km of sections dated 'Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (189-187 CE)' (a slip for 189-187 BC; Livy 39.2 dates the Via Aemilia to 187 BC); bibliography: BAtlas 39, BAtlas 40, Luccarini 2002","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Postumia","date":-147,"at":[10.64,45.23],"kind":"republic","text":"Spurius Postumius Albinus links Genua on the Tyrrhenian coast with Aquileia at the head of the Adriatic, across northern Italy.","source":"Itiner-e: 462 km of sections dated 'Spurius Postumius Albinus Magnus (148 BCE)'; bibliography: Matteazzi 2017","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Egnatia","date":-145,"at":[22.18,40.8],"kind":"republic","text":"Gnaeus Egnatius builds the road across the Balkans, through Lychnidos, Pella and Thessalonike, the land route from Italy to the East.","source":"Itiner-e: 850 km of sections dated 'Gnaeus Egnatius (146 BCE)'; bibliography: TIR Philippi, TIB 11","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Popillia","date":-131,"at":[15.82,40.05],"kind":"republic","text":"Publius Popillius Laenas carries a road from Capua down to the toe of Italy, towards Rhegium.","source":"Itiner-e: 687 km of sections dated 'Publius Popillius Laenas (132 BCE)'; bibliography: BAtlas 46","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Roads of Asia","date":-128,"at":[28.25,38.46],"kind":"republic","text":"Manius Aquilius, who organised Rome's new province of Asia, builds the first Roman roads in Anatolia, reaching south to Perge and Side.","source":"Itiner-e: 1216 km of sections dated 'Manius Aquilius (129-126 BCE)'; bibliography: RRMAM 3.1, RRMAM 3.5","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Domitia","date":-117,"at":[3.33,43.36],"kind":"republic","text":"Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus builds the road through southern Gaul, from the Pyrenees past Narbo to Nemausus: the land link between Italy and Spain.","source":"Itiner-e: 259 km of sections dated 'Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (118 BCE)'; bibliography: Laforgue Castellvi and Comps 1997, CIL 17.2","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Sebaste","date":-5,"at":[31.36,38.08],"kind":"empire","text":"Augustus has the Via Sebaste built through the mountains of Pisidia, linking his new colonies such as Antiochia in Pisidia.","source":"Itiner-e: 345 km of sections dated 'Augustus (6 BCE)'; bibliography: RRMAM 3.6, TIB 8","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Claudia Augusta","date":46,"at":[10.83,46.62],"kind":"empire","text":"The road over the Alps from the Po valley to Augusta Vindelicum (Augsburg), opened by Drusus's campaign of 15 BC, is completed under Claudius.","source":"Itiner-e: 470 km of sections dated 'Drusus-Claudius (15 BCE-46 CE)'; bibliography: Bosio 1997","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Nova Traiana","date":111,"at":[35.71,31.04],"kind":"empire","text":"After annexing Arabia, Trajan builds the Via Nova Traiana from Bostra to the Red Sea; these sections run through Madaba and Dibon.","source":"Itiner-e: 203 km of sections dated 'Trajan (111 CE)'; bibliography: Thomsen 1917, Brünnow and Domaszewski 1904","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Via Hadriana","date":137,"at":[31.52,28.03],"kind":"empire","text":"Hadrian's road runs from his new city of Antinoopolis on the Nile across the Eastern Desert to the Red Sea coast.","source":"Itiner-e: 378 km of sections dated 'Hadrian (137 CE)'; bibliography: Sidebotham and Zitterkopf 1997, Sidebotham and Zitterkopf 1998, Paprocki 2019","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"},{"title":"Strata Diocletiana","date":293,"at":[37.49,34.08],"kind":"empire","text":"Under Diocletian's Tetrarchy a military road is built along the desert frontier of Syria, through Palmyra.","source":"Itiner-e: 195 km of sections dated 'Tetrarch (293-305 CE)'; bibliography: Poidebard 1934, Bauzou 1993, Mior 2016","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17122148"}]}